{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016hgAz6Ceb2bzaSawPxr6y1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Dispute — sos_4 Together as One\n2\t\n3\t## Disputed test\n4\t`TestTogetherAsOneConvergeX::test_life_gain_goes_to_controller_not_target_player`\n5\t(`cards/sos/sos_4/tests.py`, lines 180–198)\n6\t\n7\t## Why it is internally inconsistent with the rest of the suite\n8\t\n9\tThe other tests establish an unambiguous `chosen_targets` convention:\n10\t\n11\t- **index 0 = the \"target player\" that draws X cards**\n12\t- **index 1 = the \"any target\" that takes X damage**\n13\t\n14\tEvidence:\n15\t\n16\t| Test (line) | `chosen_targets` | Assertion | Implied mapping |\n17\t|---|---|---|---|\n18\t| L124 `test_two_colors_target_player_draws_two` | `[p2, p1]` | p2 draws 2 | index 0 = draw target (p2) |\n19\t| L145 `test_three_colors_deals_three_damage_to_creature` | `[p1, bear]` | `bear.damage_marked == 3` | index 1 = damage target (bear) |\n20\t| L159 `test_two_colors_deals_two_damage_to_player` | `[p1, p2]` | `p2.life == 18` | index 1 = damage target (p2) |\n21\t| L173 `test_controller_gains_x_life` | `[p2, p2]` | `p1.life == 23` | controller (p1) gains X |\n22\t\n23\tSo damage is dealt to `chosen_targets[1]`.\n24\t\n25\tThe disputed test uses `chosen_targets = [p2, p1]` (index 1 = `p1`, the controller),\n26\tyet asserts:\n27\t\n28\t```python\n29\tassert p1.life == 22      # p1 (index 1) must NOT take damage, only gain +2\n30\tassert p2.life == 18      # p2 (index 0, the DRAW target) must take 2 damage\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tThis requires the damage target to be `chosen_targets[0]` (p2), the exact\n34\topposite of L159, which requires the damage target to be `chosen_targets[1]`.\n35\t\n36\tThere is **no single index mapping** that satisfies both L159 (`[p1, p2]` → p2\n37\tdamaged, i.e. index 1) and L191 (`[p2, p1]` → p2 damaged, i.e. index 0). The two\n38\ttests directly contradict each other.\n39\t\n40\t## Correct behavior / likely intent\n41\t\n42\tThe card text is: \"Target player draws X cards, Together as One deals X damage\n43\tto **any target**, and you gain X life.\" The intent of this specific test\n44\t(per its name and the comment \"p2 took 2 damage from being the 'any target'\")\n45\tis that the damage **any target** is `p2`, and the life gain goes to the\n46\tcontroller `p1`. For that to hold under the suite's own convention\n47\t(index 1 = damage target), the data should read:\n48\t\n49\t```python\n50\tcard.chosen_targets = [p2, p2]   # draw target = p2, damage 'any target' = p2\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\tWith `[p2, p2]` the implementation produces exactly the asserted result:\n54\tp2 draws 2 (irrelevant to the life assertions), p2 takes 2 damage → `p2.life == 18`,\n55\tand controller p1 gains 2 → `p1.life == 22`. The current literal value\n56\t`[p2, p1]` instead names the controller as the damage target, which (correctly)\n57\tnets p1 to 20, not 22.\n58\t\n59\t## Implementation status\n60\t\n61\tThe implementation (`cards/sos/sos_4/card_impl.py`) follows the suite's\n62\tdominant convention (draw = `chosen_targets[0]`, damage = `chosen_targets[1]`,\n63\tlife gain = controller) and passes **20 of 21** tests. Only the self-contradictory\n64\ttest above fails. I did not modify any test (hard rule); changing the disputed\n65\ttest's `chosen_targets` to `[p2, p2]` would make the entire suite consistent and\n66\tgreen.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb6f4bd5-6767-4f31-a917-ac0bdc13b45e","uuid":"cbf2298f-e0b7-4a3f-876d-dd495ab91a01","timestamp":"2026-06-01T08:42:10.407Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_4/test-dispute.md","content":"# Dispute — sos_4 Together as One\n\n## Disputed test\n`TestTogetherAsOneConvergeX::test_life_gain_goes_to_controller_not_target_player`\n(`cards/sos/sos_4/tests.py`, lines 180–198)\n\n## Why it is internally inconsistent with the rest of the suite\n\nThe other tests establish an unambiguous `chosen_targets` convention:\n\n- **index 0 = the \"target player\" that draws X cards**\n- **index 1 = the \"any target\" that takes X damage**\n\nEvidence:\n\n| Test (line) | `chosen_targets` | Assertion | Implied mapping |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L124 `test_two_colors_target_player_draws_two` | `[p2, p1]` | p2 draws 2 | index 0 = draw target (p2) |\n| L145 `test_three_colors_deals_three_damage_to_creature` | `[p1, bear]` | `bear.damage_marked == 3` | index 1 = damage target (bear) |\n| L159 `test_two_colors_deals_two_damage_to_player` | `[p1, p2]` | `p2.life == 18` | index 1 = damage target (p2) |\n| L173 `test_controller_gains_x_life` | `[p2, p2]` | `p1.life == 23` | controller (p1) gains X |\n\nSo damage is dealt to `chosen_targets[1]`.\n\nThe disputed test uses `chosen_targets = [p2, p1]` (index 1 = `p1`, the controller),\nyet asserts:\n\n```python\nassert p1.life == 22      # p1 (index 1) must NOT take damage, only gain +2\nassert p2.life == 18      # p2 (index 0, the DRAW target) must take 2 damage\n```\n\nThis requires the damage target to be `chosen_targets[0]` (p2), the exact\nopposite of L159, which requires the damage target to be `chosen_targets[1]`.\n\nThere is **no single index mapping** that satisfies both L159 (`[p1, p2]` → p2\ndamaged, i.e. index 1) and L191 (`[p2, p1]` → p2 damaged, i.e. index 0). The two\ntests directly contradict each other.\n\n## Correct behavior / likely intent\n\nThe card text is: \"Target player draws X cards, Together as One deals X damage\nto **any target**, and you gain X life.\" The intent of this specific test\n(per its name and the comment \"p2 took 2 damage from being the 'any target'\")\nis that the damage **any target** is `p2`, and the life gain goes to the\ncontroller `p1`. For that to hold under the suite's own convention\n(index 1 = damage target), the data should read:\n\n```python\ncard.chosen_targets = [p2, p2]   # draw target = p2, damage 'any target' = p2\n```\n\nWith `[p2, p2]` the implementation produces exactly the asserted result:\np2 draws 2 (irrelevant to the life assertions), p2 takes 2 damage → `p2.life == 18`,\nand controller p1 gains 2 → `p1.life == 22`. The current literal value\n`[p2, p1]` instead names the controller as the damage target, which (correctly)\nnets p1 to 20, not 22.\n\n## Implementation status\n\nThe implementation (`cards/sos/sos_4/card_impl.py`) follows the suite's\ndominant convention (draw = `chosen_targets[0]`, damage = `chosen_targets[1]`,\nlife gain = controller) and passes **20 of 21** tests. Only the self-contradictory\ntest above fails. I did not modify any test (hard rule); changing the disputed\ntest's `chosen_targets` to `[p2, p2]` would make the entire suite consistent and\ngreen.\n","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
