{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01PP9XCNtuB9yUFc2kjnaJz2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VyB47VqLH2pzQDxkLN9XvM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_13/review.json","content":"[\n  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 120, \"comment\": \"Oracle text and coordinator directive item 3 both specify the 'Then if an opponent controls more creatures than you' check is evaluated AFTER the token resolves. The implementation evaluates _update_prepared() BEFORE _create_inkling(). This is the correct choice given the binding test contract: test_becomes_prepared_when_opponent_has_more_creatures (p1=1 creature, p2=2, token targeted to p1) requires is_prepared==True, which after-token evaluation (2 vs 2 equal) would fail. So the before-token ordering is forced by the arbitrated test, not a free bug. However it diverges from real Oracle semantics in an uncovered case: if you and the opponent have equal creatures and you target the opponent, after-token the opponent would have strictly more and the card should become prepared, but before-token evaluation reports equal (not prepared). Noting for the record since it cannot be reconciled without changing the test; not actionable without engine/test changes that are out of scope.\"},\n  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 100, \"comment\": \"get_targets() has a dead conditional: both the _is_being_cast() branch and the fallthrough return []. The _is_being_cast() helper is therefore never observably needed for the return value. Harmless and the targeting contract test (test_no_targets_while_on_stack) passes, but the branch could be simplified to a single 'return []'.\"}\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1068,"cache_read_