{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_012yPFvWi9twpSfcyo1nn5nx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017GJ8JS4ozG85RDcjNB3QFp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_245/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 115,\n    \"comment\": \"Reducer is registered in register_triggers (called on every battlefield ENTRY at engine/zones.py:314-315) but is never unregistered when Witherbloom leaves the battlefield. The engine's leave-battlefield path (engine/zones.py:297-299) only unregisters from trigger_manager/replacement_manager; it does NOT call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(source). Consequences: (1) a stale reducer entry leaks into game.spell_cost_reducers after Witherbloom dies/leaves; (2) more seriously, on blink/recursion the card re-enters and register_triggers runs AGAIN, so two reducer entries with the same source are present, both pass the predicate, and the controller's instant/sorcery spells get DOUBLE the affinity reduction (over-reduction). The implementer added unregister_spell_cost_reducer but never wired it to a leave hook, and register_triggers is not idempotent. Fix: unregister the prior reducer before (or instead of re-)registering (e.g. call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(self) at the top of register_triggers, or hook a leave-battlefield callback). The _is_on_battlefield re-check prevents value errors only for a single registration; it does not prevent the double-count when two identical entries exist.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 40,\n    \"comment\": \"Helpers (_count_creatures, _is_on_battlefield) swallow all exceptions with bare `except Exception`. This is defensive but can mask genuine engine bugs (e.g. an AttributeError from a renamed battlefield API) by silently returning 0/False, which would make the affinity reduction silently disappear instead of failing loudly. Consider narrowing the caught exception types.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1021,"cache_read_input_tokens":23271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1021,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JjmHBjGEyJ1mjacdFiQTo1","session_id":"eb6f4bd5-6767-4f31-a917-ac0bdc13b45e","uuid":"d6c01f36-dbe4-4517-9759-ca4aceb1105f","request_id":"req_011CbcMeBsb2eErd2ocMc8Te","subagent_type":"Reviewer","task_description":"Review sos_245"}
