{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MvWh8PZokr7ifpU7n1jfLi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 115,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"Reducer is registered in register_triggers (called on every battlefield ENTRY at engine/zones.py:314-315) but is never unregistered when Witherbloom leaves the battlefield. The engine's leave-battlefield path (engine/zones.py:297-299) only unregisters from trigger_manager/replacement_manager; it does NOT call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(source). Consequences: (1) a stale reducer entry leaks into game.spell_cost_reducers after Witherbloom dies/leaves; (2) more seriously, on blink/recursion the card re-enters and register_triggers runs AGAIN, so two reducer entries with the same source are present, both pass the predicate, and the controller's instant/sorcery spells get DOUBLE the affinity reduction (over-reduction). The implementer added unregister_spell_cost_reducer but never wired it to a leave hook, and register_triggers is not idempotent. Fix: unregister the prior reducer before (or instead of re-)registering (e.g. call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(self) at the top of register_triggers, or hook a leave-battlefield callback). The _is_on_battlefield re-check prevents value errors only for a single registration; it does not prevent the double-count when two identical entries exist.\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 40,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"Helpers (_count_creatures, _is_on_battlefield) swallow all exceptions with bare `except Exception`. This is defensive but can mask genuine engine bugs (e.g. an AttributeError from a renamed battlefield API) by silently returning 0/False, which would make the affinity reduction silently disappear instead of failing loudly. Consider narrowing the caught exception types.\"\n13\t  }\n14\t]\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb6f4bd5-6767-4f31-a917-ac0bdc13b45e","uuid":"1901b211-4839-466e-a479-bb6bc432c2f5","timestamp":"2026-06-01T10:10:31.605Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_245/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 115,\n    \"comment\": \"Reducer is registered in register_triggers (called on every battlefield ENTRY at engine/zones.py:314-315) but is never unregistered when Witherbloom leaves the battlefield. The engine's leave-battlefield path (engine/zones.py:297-299) only unregisters from trigger_manager/replacement_manager; it does NOT call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(source). Consequences: (1) a stale reducer entry leaks into game.spell_cost_reducers after Witherbloom dies/leaves; (2) more seriously, on blink/recursion the card re-enters and register_triggers runs AGAIN, so two reducer entries with the same source are present, both pass the predicate, and the controller's instant/sorcery spells get DOUBLE the affinity reduction (over-reduction). The implementer added unregister_spell_cost_reducer but never wired it to a leave hook, and register_triggers is not idempotent. Fix: unregister the prior reducer before (or instead of re-)registering (e.g. call game.unregister_spell_cost_reducer(self) at the top of register_triggers, or hook a leave-battlefield callback). The _is_on_battlefield re-check prevents value errors only for a single registration; it does not prevent the double-count when two identical entries exist.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 40,\n    \"comment\": \"Helpers (_count_creatures, _is_on_battlefield) swallow all exceptions with bare `except Exception`. This is defensive but can mask genuine engine bugs (e.g. an AttributeError from a renamed battlefield API) by silently returning 0/False, which would make the affinity reduction silently disappear instead of failing loudly. Consider narrowing the caught exception types.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":15,"startLine":1,"totalLines":15}}}
